          </div>
      </Figure>
    </Compare>

    <p class="group-label">The closeout, in decisions</p>
    <p>
      The review screen wasn't the only place the interface and the contract disagreed. A
      closeout audit covered the workflows around it — access, intake, the queue, CSV limits,
      cleanup — and each finding became a written decision: a rationale, an owner, a
      verification path, and a status.
    </p>
    <div class="declog">
      <div class="declog-row">
        <div>
          <p class="declog-title">Access applies only on an explicit action</p>
          <p class="declog-why">
            The role-to-access mapping stays reviewable and editable; nothing is applied until
            a person presses “Apply approved mapping.”
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="declog-status">In release</span>
      </div>
      <div class="declog-row">
        <div>
          <p class="declog-title">One file at a time, said out loud</p>
          <p class="declog-why">
            The CSV limit is announced before selection, and extra files are rejected with
            guidance instead of silently truncated. Mapping per file stays a follow-up, not a
            surprise.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="declog-status">In release</span>
      </div>
      <div class="declog-row">
        <div>
          <p class="declog-title">Call the queue what it is</p>
          <p class="declog-why">
            Renamed Review queue and scoped to the provider administrators who can actually use
            it. No implied supervisor role, no dead end for the people the label promised.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="declog-status">In release</span>
      </div>
      <div class="declog-row">
        <div>
          <p class="declog-title">Archive is the soft delete</p>
          <p class="declog-why">
            Facilities and client namespaces archive and restore; grants, documents, evidence,
            and audit history stay. No new table, no migration, no provider-facing hard delete.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="declog-status">In release</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p class="study-disclosure">
      Code and tests are in place across these four decisions; the representative-role
      walkthroughs are still pending. I'm leaving that gap visible rather than claiming a
      sign-off the team hasn't recorded yet.
    </p>

    <p class="group-label">What's on the table next</p>
    <p>
      Two more decisions are written and waiting on design-system approval. They aren't
      shipped — they show where the same principles are pulling next.
    </p>
    <div class="declog">
      <div class="declog-row">
        <div>
          <p class="declog-title">A record deserves a page, not a drawer</p>
          <p class="declog-why">
            Inbound receiving is a long, multi-step review. It moves from a right-side drawer to
            a full record page with a URL, refresh-safe state, and a line table wider than a
            modal allows. Rejected: a wider drawer, a bigger modal, an expand button.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="declog-status declog-status-pending">Proposed</span>
      </div>
      <div class="declog-row">
        <div>
          <p class="declog-title">One filter anatomy across six catalog tabs</p>
          <p class="declog-why">
            Products, variants, inventory, BOM, routing, and reconciliation get one stable
            block: the same three bands in the same order, quick filters and actions on one
            line, a sheet on mobile. No per-tab height jumps.
          </p>
        </div>
        <span class="declog-status declog-status-pending">Proposed</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </Act>

  <Act num="06" title="Impact & status">
    <p>
      Recently shipped — and I'll be precise about that. The four review surfaces became one; the
      queue leads with exceptions and derives one action per row; intake terminology is locked
      across the workspace. Then the review got a second pass: Review and Approve separated into
      a workbench and a decision, and the review tables became editable in place. Commercial
      billing went from a hand-rolled page to a governed flow with confirmations on every
      irreversible step. And the platform's design system now ships in versioned releases with
      automated guardrail tests, so the patterns hold across a workspace that spans intake,
      receiving, dispatch, inventory, assembly, billing, and returns.
    </p>
    <div class="counted">
      <div class="counted-item">
        <span class="counted-num">4 → 1</span>
        <span class="counted-label">review surfaces consolidated</span>
      </div>
      <div class="counted-item">
        <span class="counted-num">11</span>
        <span class="counted-label">friction points resolved</span>
      </div>
      <div class="counted-item">
        <span class="counted-num">0</span>
        <span class="counted-label">new backend endpoints required</span>
      </div>
      <div class="counted-item">
        <span class="counted-num">2</span>
        <span class="counted-label">confirmations, deliberately placed</span>
      </div>
      <div class="counted-item">
        <span class="counted-num">Green</span>
        <span class="counted-label">full suite, guardrails included</span>
      </div>
      <div class="counted-item">
        <span class="counted-num">0</span>
        <span class="counted-label">editor modals in the review</span>
      </div>
    </div>
    <p>
      What doesn't exist yet is operator outcome data: the newest flows shipped weeks ago, and
      I'd rather show you the structure than a number I can't defend. The intake structure is
      checkable in the screenshots above. The review split shipped for receiving evidence and
      spreadsheet BOMs; the PDF consolidation is still rolling out.
    </p>
  </Act>

  <Act num="07" title="What I'd do differently">
    <p>
      I'd have separated editing from deciding on day one. The one-surface rule was right, but a
      workbench and a decision are different tools; giving both tabs the same editable components
      made both slower. The second pass fixed the shape — after the operators had taught us where
      the seam was.
    </p>
    <p>
      I'd lock terminology in week one. Doing it at consolidation meant re-teaching every screen
      that already existed.
    </p>
    <p>
      And “show ready” should have been the default fold from the start. We knew attention should
      follow risk, but keeping ready records visible felt safer to stakeholders. The operators
      never needed them. Attention-first is the right default — everywhere.
    </p>
  </Act>

